#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# list_interpolation

#% text
# `list_interpolation(array)` gives a function that interpolates the values of an array given at the integer coordinates 1, 2, ... in each dimension.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `list_interpolation(array, [[xmin, xmax], ...])` puts the values at equally spaced coordinates from `xmin` to `xmax`; `list_interpolation(array, [[x1, x2, ...], ...])` at the coordinates given.

#% item
# Between the nodes the function is the Lagrange polynomial of degree `interpolation_order` (default 3) through the nearest nodes; in several dimensions it is applied along each dimension in turn.

#% item
# `interpolation_order` may be a list, one order per dimension; order 0 takes the nearest value.

#% item
# In one dimension the data may also be pairs `[[x1], v1], [[x2], v2], ...`; a value given as `[f, f', ...]` uses `f`.

#% item
# The function returns numbers at numeric arguments and stays unevaluated at symbolic ones, so that it can be plotted.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
f = list_interpolation([1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 5])

#% cell
f(2.5)
#% output
#= 2.4375

#% cell
g = list_interpolation([1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 5], [[0, 1]])

#% cell
g(0.5)
#% output
#= 3.874999999999999

#% cell
h = list_interpolation([[1, 2, 3, 4], [2, 2, 6, 4], [3, 6, 3, 12], [4, 4, 12, 4]])

#% cell
h(2.5, 2.5)
#% output
#= 3.953125

#% cell
k = list_interpolation([1, 5, 7, 2, 3, 1], [[0, 1]], interpolation_order: 0)

#% cell
[k(0.1), k(0.5)]
#% output
#= [5, 2]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `interpolation`, `function_interpolation`, `interpolating_polynomial`
